that on the website,

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:03.820
there is more details there.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.600
So when we think particularly
around disaster recovery

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.479
and storage for Google, I
think there's four key terms

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:11.790
that we are trying
to design for;

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:14.412
trust, secure,
smart, and simple.

00:02:14.412 --> 00:02:15.870
So if we go into
that, for example,

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.196
with Google Cloud Platform,
one of the things you have

00:02:18.196 --> 00:02:20.070
to do in order to trust
someone is make sure,

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:24.510
if you're going to put all
of your files or your object

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.060
storage into Google, is you
trust this will actually

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.010
be available when you need it.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.860
And so this is some
data from Gartner

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.152
looking at the
downtime of storage

00:02:34.152 --> 00:02:35.610
from the different
cloud providers.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.920
And hopefully we're
recognizing that we

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.729
will be there when you need us.

00:02:39.729 --> 00:02:41.520
In addition, there are
a few other features

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.400
that I think differentiate when
we're looking at cloud storage

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:45.430
options.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:47.300
One is a single consistent
API, so you don't

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.290
have to manage multiple APIs.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.540
And that API actually works
with other cloud providers

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.070
as well, which
makes it really easy

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.860
to move data, edge
caching, given our network,

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:59.550
making sure that the data can be
easily brought to you quickly,

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:02.040
consistency and things
around security,

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.710
like default encryption,
that's automatically

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.080
encrypt data at
rest and in transit,

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.160
as well as object
lifecycle management.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:12.390
So as you look at, for
example, our different buckets

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:15.650
of storage, going from
hot and multi-regional

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:20.280
all the way on the left to cold,
which is really just archive,

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:24.120
we can really easily help
you move your storage

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.340
through that process
automatically

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:27.629
through lifecycle management.

00:03:27.629 --> 00:03:29.670
The benefit of that to
you is that, if you're not

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.794
touching something, you
can move it down in a class

00:03:31.794 --> 00:03:33.270
and it will save you some money.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.659
So as we go through
this process--

00:03:35.659 --> 00:03:37.200
and I'm not going
to read through all

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.991
these different buckets--
but just remember

00:03:38.991 --> 00:03:41.880
that we have many different
scenarios and different options

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.750
to match what you need
for disaster recovery.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.125
So as we go from
there, I'm going

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:48.900
to introduce three
of our speakers.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:50.584
We'll have Arun
from Stanford, who

00:03:50.584 --> 00:03:52.750
will be talking about how
they're using Google Cloud

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:54.930
Platform for their
disaster recovery, Steve

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:57.388
from University of Minnesota,
who will be talking about how

00:03:57.388 --> 00:03:59.880
they're using G Suite for
a lot of their file backup,

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.580
and Joshua from Georgia State,
also talking about how they're

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:03.911
using Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:03.911 --> 00:04:05.660
And then at the end,
we'll take questions.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:08.035
So if you have any questions,
feel free to jot them down.

00:04:08.035 --> 00:04:11.040
We have a microphone, and I will
