00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:37.240
As an introduction, I'd like
you to look at this picture.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.245
It's a beautiful picture
of San Francisco.

00:04:39.245 --> 00:04:42.700
It's got the cityscape in the
background, the fog rolling in,

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:45.159
and the very famous Golden
Gate Bridge peeking out.

00:04:45.159 --> 00:04:46.950
STEVEN BUTSCHI: I'm
going to give you this.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.875
There you go.

00:04:49.875 --> 00:04:51.000
ARUN AGGARAWAL: Is that on?

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.110
OK.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:54.670
And the very famous Golden
Gate Bridge peeking out.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:04:59.700
San Francisco is truly
a beautiful city.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.283
And I hope in the
last few days you've

00:05:01.283 --> 00:05:03.960
been able to take a few
minutes to look around, enjoy

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:05.610
the sights and scenes.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.562
But San Francisco is
known for one other thing,

00:05:08.562 --> 00:05:11.040
and that's earthquakes.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:14.580
We who live here know about
these natural disasters

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.490
very well.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:20.660
This entire area is situated
on six major fault lines

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:23.450
and a slew of smaller ones.

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:27.700
In fact, geologists predict
that, in the next 25 years,

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:32.560
there is at least a 70% chance
of a significant quake hitting

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:35.526
on one of these faults.

00:05:35.526 --> 00:05:37.250
It could happen tomorrow.

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.280
It could happen next year.

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:41.440
You don't know.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.340
But what we do know is the
damage that earthquakes can do.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:49.940
Back in 1989, we
had a 6.9 earthquake

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:53.340
that hit San Francisco,
the whole area.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:55.430
It was dubbed the
World Series earthquake

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:58.790
because it happened just moments
before game three of the World

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:05.210
Series in 1989 that was
between the San Francisco

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:08.040
Giants and the Oakland
A's, just across the Bay.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.630
And you can see
what damage it did--

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:13.510
a big gaping hole
in the Bay Bridge,

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:17.020
a downed building in one
San Francisco neighborhood.

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:19.300
Both of these were
just moments away

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:22.040
from where we're
sitting right now.

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:24.490
So let's bring this back around.

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:26.620
If you, as an IT
professional, are

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:30.670
responsible for your critical
data for your organization

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:33.610
and you happen to live in
an earthquake-prone area,

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:36.250
or any place that
has disasters, you

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.380
want to make sure that
that data is safe.

00:06:41.794 --> 00:06:43.210
This morning, I'd
like to take you

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:48.290
through our journey of creating
a disaster recovery plan.

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:52.280
In doing so, I'll talk
about three major topics;

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:55.640
why we chose the Cloud,
why we chose Google,

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:59.090
and how we achieved disaster
recovery with zero downtime.

00:07:02.874 --> 00:07:05.090
The Graduate School of
Business at Stanford

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:10.020
has over 200 terabytes of
critical research data.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:14.510
This data is sitting
on our NAS, on premise,

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:20.730
on storage disk from a
major storage vendor.

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:28.590
Our faculty researchers use
this data to create and publish

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:31.620
major papers on
topics from anywhere

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:35.520
from international
economics to human behavior.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.100
This research is
world-renowned, and it's

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:43.920
critical to attracting top
talent to the university,

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:45.195
both faculty and students.

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:51.480
Amazingly, as of
just two years ago,

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:54.640
this data was not
even backed up.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.250
Clearly, we needed a
disaster recovery plan.

00:07:57.250 --> 00:08:01.440
And we needed one
implemented very quickly.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:05.080
As we talked about it, many
requirements came to mind.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:07.320
We wanted to make sure
that our disaster recovery

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.980
plan was geodiverse.

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:11.570
As I've just been
talking about, we

00:08:11.570 --> 00:08:13.790
didn't want an earthquake
to hit a data center

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:19.170
and take out our NAS and
RDR solution all at once.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:21.770
Our solution had to
be highly available.

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:23.720
Our solution had to be scalable.

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:26.120
As our data footprint grew,
we wanted the solution

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:29.840
to grow with it without
any major rearchitecture.

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:32.450
And we needed quick
recovery times

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:34.069
in the event of a disaster.

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:40.210
As we did our basic
research, it was clear,

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:45.290
and we were appointed very
quickly to using the cloud.

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:48.740
It was the most efficient,
the most cost effective way

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:50.160
of achieving these
various goals.

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:56.970
Add to that the fact that
the university had recently

00:08:56.970 --> 00:09:00.570
printed its Cloud
Vision Statement, in it,

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:02.790
it stated that the
university wanted

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:07.080
to move the majority of its IT
infrastructure out to the cloud

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:09.310
by 2019.

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:11.130
That's just next year.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:14.210
But that confirmed the
direction that we were going.

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:16.670
I'll let you read Stanford's
Cloud Vision Statement, which

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:18.150
is up here on the screen.

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:23.500
And it will, again, point out
the key words; cost-efficiency,

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:26.957
scalability, and accessibility.

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:41.440
Cost, of course, is a big
factor on any major initiative,

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:45.544
especially when you're dealing
with university budgets.

00:09:45.544 --> 00:09:47.710
In our case, when we started
thinking about disaster

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:52.760
recovery, we thought, hey, why
not just bring in another NAS,

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:55.760
bring it on premise, copy all
the bits from our production

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:58.610
NAS to the second
NAS, and thereby we

00:09:58.610 --> 00:10:00.770
would have our solution?

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:04.670
We talked to our storage vendor,
and they sent us a quote.

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:09.480
The quote came in at $137,000--

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:12.500
not chump change for us.

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:15.800
Further, that didn't include
the installation costs,

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:19.460
nor did it include the
system administration costs

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:21.620
that we would need to
bear over the lifetime

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:24.540
of this new solution.

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:27.900
Google Cloud Storage,
by comparison,

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:30.270
had minimal upfront costs.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:32.740
And it billed monthly,
depending, of course,

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:35.450
on the size of your
data footprint.

00:10:35.450 --> 00:10:39.050
We estimated that, in the
first year, that would come out

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:42.290
to around $60,000 for us.

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:44.095
You can see the big
price difference.

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:49.560
Beyond cost, there
were many other reasons

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:51.680
as to why we chose Google.

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:55.700
Our university had already a
preexisting business agreement

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:57.460
with Google.

00:10:57.460 --> 00:11:02.500
And in that agreement, we
could store high-risk data out

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:05.560
into GCS, and even PHI data.

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:07.800
That was important to us.

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:13.140
Second, we had a fantastic team
assigned to us from Google.

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:15.360
Once they learned
about our project,

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:19.640
they worked hard to find the
right solution for our DR.

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:22.780
They communicated
with us continuously.

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:26.110
And as a matter of fact, we met
at this very same conference

00:11:26.110 --> 00:11:27.670
a year ago.

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:31.190
I was able to sit down with
them, talk about our needs,

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:33.490
talk about our requirements.

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:34.840
They took lots of notes.

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:36.310
They went back.

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:38.140
They figured out some
possible solutions.

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:40.350
And they came back quickly.

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:42.570
Once we talked about
it, we discovered

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:44.370
that there was a
particular Google

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:48.165
partner that would potentially
serve our purpose very well.

00:11:48.165 --> 00:11:50.040
I'd like to go into in
a little bit of detail

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:51.290
about that particular partner.

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:57.090
That was a company
called Komprise.

00:11:57.090 --> 00:11:59.730
Komprise is a company
and a software

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:02.490
that allows you to
analyze, move, and manage

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:08.110
your data across all your
disk volumes and, in fact,

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:13.090
across object stores
out in the cloud.

00:12:13.090 --> 00:12:16.300
Together with GCS,
the Komprise software

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:19.750
provided for as a complete
DR solution, and one

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:22.430
that we have in place today.

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:25.070
The company itself was
wonderful to work with.

00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:30.890
They developed a collaborative
relationship with us.

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:32.900
They were eager to
hear our feedback.

00:12:32.900 --> 00:12:35.300
We were eager to give
them our feedback.

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:38.736
And in fact, much of that was
introduced into future versions

00:12:38.736 --> 00:12:39.485
of their software.

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:44.550
That's all fine and good.

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:46.860
But what really
sold us on Komprise

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:50.756
was the capabilities
of their software.

00:12:50.756 --> 00:12:52.130
From this picture,
I hope you can

00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:55.220
see that we were
able to replicate all

00:12:55.220 --> 00:13:00.520
200 terabytes of our
research data out to GCS.

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:02.890
Moreover-- and
this is important--

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:09.900
the files came over while
preserving all file attributes.

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:15.190
More specifically, it preserved
all security file attributes.

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:18.080
And even more
specifically, it preserved

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:21.910
NFSv4 security file attributes.

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:26.290
We came to learn that NFSv4
is not highly supported,

00:13:26.290 --> 00:13:27.880
but it was critical
to our solution.

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:34.200
Once all of our
data was out at GCS,

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:35.740
the Komprise
software then allowed

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:40.710
us to actually mount that
Google GCS bucket back

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:44.620
to our on-prem compute hardware.

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:49.240
I hope you can see then that
if our NAS ever went out,

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:52.240
we could simply
mount the GCS bucket,

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:56.890
and we would be up and
running with our data

00:13:56.890 --> 00:13:59.634
with zero downtime.

00:13:59.634 --> 00:14:01.800
As they say, that's exactly
what the doctor ordered.

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:07.090
In conclusion then,
I want to state again

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:11.680
how important it was for us
to achieve disaster recovery.

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:14.350
We did it with GCS
and with Komprise.

00:14:14.350 --> 00:14:18.250
And together, they formed a
solid solution that, again,

00:14:18.250 --> 00:14:21.110
is in place for us today.

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:23.780
Looking ahead, now
that all of our data

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:27.710
is safely out into the
cloud, we could imagine, hey,

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:30.920
maybe instead of having
our on-premise data,

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:34.070
maybe our primary storer
should be out in the cloud.

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:36.400
And if we did that, we could
then take the next step

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:39.670
and think about, hey, maybe
we should put our compute out

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:41.790
in the cloud.

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:44.940
In essence, we would have Google
do all of the infrastructure

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:48.710
heavy lifting for us,
and we could concentrate

00:14:48.710 --> 00:14:51.860
on what value-add
we're there for, namely

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:55.620
the data, data analysis, data
integration, and that sort

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:56.120
of thing.

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:03.320
Putting all of that compute out
into the cloud also minimizes

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:07.340
our on-prem footprint,
which is a very good thing--

00:15:07.340 --> 00:15:13.130
no servers, no
administration, no headaches.

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:16.190
And when you live here in
earthquake-prone country,

00:15:16.190 --> 00:15:18.260
as I was talking about,
you don't want to mess

00:15:18.260 --> 00:15:22.760
around with losing your
data and your compute.

00:15:22.760 --> 00:15:24.140
Thanks very much.

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:25.846
I'm going to hand
it over to Steven.

00:15:25.846 --> 00:15:27.750
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:34.189
STEVE NGUYEN: Thank you, Arun.

00:15:34.189 --> 00:15:34.730
Hi, everyone.

00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:36.430
My name is Steve Nguyen,
and I'm with the University

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:37.130
of Minnesota.

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:39.421
I'm the service owner for
collaboration and web content

00:15:39.421 --> 00:15:40.540
services.

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:43.360
The University of Minnesota is
comprised of five main campuses

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:45.700
across the state of
Minnesota, the Twin Cities

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:46.450
being the largest.

00:15:46.450 --> 00:15:49.840
We have about 100,000 students
and approximately 35,000

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:51.790
faculty and staff.

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:55.690
My role here is to ensure
that the service is delivered

00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:57.910
through operations,
process improvement,

00:15:57.910 --> 00:15:59.380
and through
strategic [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:02.530
in the collaboration
web content space.

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:08.380
What I want to talk about next
is the University of Minnesota

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:10.720
and how we have
200 million files

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:13.900
and 2.3 petabytes in the cloud.

00:16:13.900 --> 00:16:17.950
We're ready for storms,
tornadoes, and even

00:16:17.950 --> 00:16:19.390
the occasional squirrel.

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:21.340
And I'll explain that one later.

00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:24.070
While we don't have
earthquakes like Arun did,

00:16:24.070 --> 00:16:26.387
we have other things that
cause us to think about DR.

00:16:26.387 --> 00:16:28.720
And so I want to walk you
through how we've done it here

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:32.480
at the University and
how you can do the same.

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:35.700
The University has been an early
adopter of Google since 2009.

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:38.310
But we didn't all jump right in.

00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:41.637
As I look back to those
days in the late 2000s,

00:16:41.637 --> 00:16:43.470
we were contemplating
about moving to Google

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:45.550
as a collaboration platform.

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:49.217
We had pockets of users
using Google and wanting

00:16:49.217 --> 00:16:51.300
leadership and others to
move into this direction.

00:16:51.300 --> 00:16:54.720
But the rest of the campus
didn't have an appetite for it.

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:57.250
Faculty were concerned
about storage,

00:16:57.250 --> 00:16:59.250
how they access their
files, where they find it.

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:03.540
Administration were concerned
about security, performance,

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:04.890
and uptime.

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:06.900
But all in all, the
questions really

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:08.710
boiled down to two things.

00:17:08.710 --> 00:17:11.960
One, can we trust Google?

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:15.210
And two, will
Google be reliable?

00:17:15.210 --> 00:17:18.109
So through a series of town
halls with stakeholders

00:17:18.109 --> 00:17:19.910
and with leadership,
we concluded

00:17:19.910 --> 00:17:22.220
that the benefits in
the cloud just far

00:17:22.220 --> 00:17:24.380
outweighed those concerns.

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:27.859
For example, we used
to use Word docs.

00:17:27.859 --> 00:17:30.260
You know how you create
them, you send them off,

00:17:30.260 --> 00:17:32.480
someone would download
them, make changes,

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:33.880
send them right back?

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:36.200
Well, since we've
moved to Google Docs,

00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:39.890
the experience for our users
has been just tremendous.

00:17:39.890 --> 00:17:44.180
They now have in any time,
anywhere, and any device

00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:46.730
real-time collaboration.

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:49.790
We also wanted to strive
after a high-availability

00:17:49.790 --> 00:17:51.800
infrastructure.

00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:54.270
So we asked ourselves,
as an institution,

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:56.660
how do we minimize the
downtime that students, staff,

00:17:56.660 --> 00:18:00.250
and faculty have in
using these products?

00:18:00.250 --> 00:18:04.070
And so we were after high
availability to the five nines.

00:18:04.070 --> 00:18:07.400
And I think we have pretty
much achieved that with Google.

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:11.010
But the largest benefit,
however, is in the savings.

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:13.760
And that's what I want
to talk about next.

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:16.060
The savings we achieved
from going to Google

00:18:16.060 --> 00:18:18.570
were in time and money.

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:22.850
We used to spend a lot of
time managing servers--

00:18:22.850 --> 00:18:25.510
patching them, monitoring
them, troubleshooting them.

00:18:25.510 --> 00:18:27.710
You know how that works.

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:29.514
And usually we would
get a vendor patch,

00:18:29.514 --> 00:18:31.430
and we applied them, and
we think that they've

00:18:31.430 --> 00:18:32.305
tested it thoroughly.

00:18:32.305 --> 00:18:32.810
They didn't.

00:18:32.810 --> 00:18:33.640
We applied them.

00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:36.620
Of course, disruption
and disaster, right?

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:39.290
Well, since moving
our files to Google,

00:18:39.290 --> 00:18:41.619
we don't have to worry
about that anymore.

00:18:41.619 --> 00:18:43.410
Google does a better
job of it than we can.

00:18:43.410 --> 00:18:45.030
So why not let them manage it?

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:49.100
So our sysadmin is now really
focusing on other priorities

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:53.090
where their expertise
is needed in that area.

00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:55.270
The other benefit
that we mentioned

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:58.550
were the savings
in terms of money.

00:18:58.550 --> 00:19:01.630
We asked Gartner to come in
and do an independent analysis

00:19:01.630 --> 00:19:04.780
of what the savings would
be if the University moved

00:19:04.780 --> 00:19:08.140
to an all-cloud collaboration
platform versus building

00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:09.940
something on-prem.

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:12.130
They determined that
the university would

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:15.291
save approximately $15 million.

00:19:15.291 --> 00:19:17.086
For those in higher
education, you

00:19:17.086 --> 00:19:20.040
know that's not a small number.

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:22.290
And it's always a decreasing
budget every year,

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:24.800
right, so we're asked
to do more with less.

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:27.160
And so my question to
you is, what could you

00:19:27.160 --> 00:19:30.850
do with $15 million
in that savings?

00:19:30.850 --> 00:19:33.325
Well, what we did was we
went to administration

00:19:33.325 --> 00:19:35.740
and went to faculty,
and we said to them,

00:19:35.740 --> 00:19:37.790
here's something you
don't hear every day.

00:19:37.790 --> 00:19:39.040
Here's $15 million for you.

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:41.870
What do you want to do with it?

00:19:41.870 --> 00:19:43.900
They came back, and
they said we want

00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:49.150
this, a collaborative teaching
and learning classroom.

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:53.510
This is where the University
invested the savings.

00:19:53.510 --> 00:19:57.580
This building here houses 10 of
the high-tech active learning

00:19:57.580 --> 00:19:59.430
classrooms that
we have on campus.

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:01.930
For those who aren't familiar
with what high active learning

00:20:01.930 --> 00:20:04.090
classrooms are,
they are classroom

00:20:04.090 --> 00:20:06.580
designed to offer a
flexible environment that

00:20:06.580 --> 00:20:11.500
encourages group work, class
participation, and increased

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:13.600
participation.

00:20:13.600 --> 00:20:16.000
Students here are working
on projects together.

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:18.640
They're collaborating, sharing
their results within each other

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:20.223
and the classroom
through technologies

00:20:20.223 --> 00:20:22.990
implemented in that building.

00:20:22.990 --> 00:20:26.140
This facility is also
a big draw on campus,

00:20:26.140 --> 00:20:27.740
not only for teaching
and learning,

00:20:27.740 --> 00:20:30.550
but for also other events that
need a collaborative learning

00:20:30.550 --> 00:20:31.564
space.

00:20:31.564 --> 00:20:32.980
Steven can recall
that he was here

00:20:32.980 --> 00:20:36.280
at this same place in March
for our Google Day event,

00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:40.950
using the facilities and
the equipment in this room.

00:20:40.950 --> 00:20:43.270
But these students,
however, have

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:49.360
an ever-increasing expectation
of availability and reliability

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:51.280
on the Enterprise
applications that we

00:20:51.280 --> 00:20:52.880
have in our data center.

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:56.290
And so I want to take a look
at two examples of where

00:20:56.290 --> 00:21:02.962
the impact of those data
centers caused a DR situation.

00:21:02.962 --> 00:21:04.920
The picture on your left
shows you a large body

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:06.480
of Minnesota water.

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:08.520
And the picture on
your right shows

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:11.370
you one of the
University's data center.

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:16.080
Imagine all this water
flooding into that data center.

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:19.362
When that happened, obviously
our files and servers

00:21:19.362 --> 00:21:20.070
were unavailable.

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:24.030
And our users were very
upset, and they didn't

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:25.642
want this to happen again.

00:21:25.642 --> 00:21:27.600
So we thought about how
we would mitigate this.

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:29.400
And so we said, we
need to have a cloud

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:32.820
file storage solution that would
allow the users to continue

00:21:32.820 --> 00:21:34.800
to access their
files and store them

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:37.200
without having to
worry about a flood,

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:40.240
because moving the data
center was not an option.

00:21:40.240 --> 00:21:43.690
And so what we wanted
to do was we looked

00:21:43.690 --> 00:21:47.430
and moved to the files that
we had to Google Drive.

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:49.630
And so our most
recent flood wasn't

00:21:49.630 --> 00:21:51.040
as severe as the first one.

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:53.980
But when it happened, we
were already in the cloud

00:21:53.980 --> 00:21:59.240
and we were unaffected by
that from a user standpoint.

00:21:59.240 --> 00:22:03.000
My next DR example occurred
when we had an important upgrade

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:06.540
that we went live with.

00:22:06.540 --> 00:22:08.280
We had a complex,
year-long project

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:11.070
to upgrade a key
business application.

00:22:11.070 --> 00:22:13.490
And the upgrade was
complete, and we

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:15.740
were ready for the launch.

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:19.820
On the day of the launch,
one of the transformers

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:23.930
powering our data center
blew up and caught fire.

00:22:23.930 --> 00:22:28.040
And obviously this
delayed our launch.

00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:30.980
Anybody care to guess what
caused that transformer

00:22:30.980 --> 00:22:32.555
to catch fire?

00:22:32.555 --> 00:22:33.860
AUDIENCE: Squirrel.

00:22:33.860 --> 00:22:33.972
STEVE NGUYEN: Yeah.

00:22:33.972 --> 00:22:34.472
Good.

00:22:34.472 --> 00:22:36.139
You guys were paying attention.

00:22:36.139 --> 00:22:36.680
That's right.

00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:38.900
So hard to believe,
but a squirrel somehow

00:22:38.900 --> 00:22:40.910
found its way into
the transformer,

00:22:40.910 --> 00:22:44.210
built its nest or whatever,
and caused the fire

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:47.300
to take down our data center.

00:22:47.300 --> 00:22:48.290
Yeah.

00:22:48.290 --> 00:22:51.610
This caused IT to
have a huge black eye.

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:53.630
So all credibility
and trust with IT

00:22:53.630 --> 00:22:55.520
went out the window that day.

00:22:55.520 --> 00:22:57.740
It took us a long time to
rebuild that relationship

00:22:57.740 --> 00:23:00.950
and earn their trust again
in our partnership with them.

00:23:04.084 --> 00:23:05.500
You can't really
plan for anything

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:06.490
that's unexpected, right?

00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:07.930
And here are two
examples of that.

00:23:07.930 --> 00:23:10.330
I can share more of just
some of these crazy stories

00:23:10.330 --> 00:23:12.072
that you just can't plan for.

00:23:12.072 --> 00:23:13.780
But by leveraging
Google, what we did was

00:23:13.780 --> 00:23:17.530
we took all of our files
that everybody was using

00:23:17.530 --> 00:23:19.240
and put them in the
cloud to prepare

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:24.880
and plan for these unplanned
events that happened.

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:27.490
Like our users,
this squirrel here

00:23:27.490 --> 00:23:30.310
was just looking for a safe
place to store its nuts, right?

00:23:30.310 --> 00:23:32.680
So isn't that what
we're here, too,

00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:35.030
to learn about how we can
securely store our files?

00:23:37.162 --> 00:23:38.870
Now that we talked a
little bit about DR,

00:23:38.870 --> 00:23:42.590
I want to switch gears and
just talk about Team Drives.

00:23:42.590 --> 00:23:47.310
Imagine a scenario
with me for a second.

00:23:47.310 --> 00:23:49.710
You have a staff person
who's been with your place

00:23:49.710 --> 00:23:52.630
for seven years, creating
content and so on.

00:23:52.630 --> 00:23:55.830
They're ready to move
on now, and now it's

00:23:55.830 --> 00:24:00.010
time for them to go.

00:24:00.010 --> 00:24:02.130
How does an organization
effectively offboard

00:24:02.130 --> 00:24:05.160
that person while
retaining these documents

00:24:05.160 --> 00:24:08.400
and make sure that they
don't leave with that person?

00:24:08.400 --> 00:24:12.170
How does your organization
do that right now?

00:24:12.170 --> 00:24:15.410
Well, prior to Team Drives,
whenever a staff person left,

00:24:15.410 --> 00:24:17.240
they had this daunting
task ahead of them

00:24:17.240 --> 00:24:18.980
which they didn't want to do.

00:24:18.980 --> 00:24:20.480
And that is that
their teams needed

00:24:20.480 --> 00:24:23.300
them to change ownership of
all the working documents

00:24:23.300 --> 00:24:26.680
that they had and assign
that to a new person.

00:24:26.680 --> 00:24:29.560
And this was a process
that was very haphazard.

00:24:29.560 --> 00:24:31.194
It was painful for the business.

00:24:31.194 --> 00:24:32.610
The staff person
who was departing

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:33.860
really didn't want to do this.

00:24:33.860 --> 00:24:34.680
I've got another week left.

00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:37.230
I'm not going to sit here and
spend hours trying to change

00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:38.490
ownership of documents.

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:42.270
And so we would usually have to
contact that person after they

00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:44.910
left and ask them to
reassign these documents

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:47.040
to another person.

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:49.470
Well, Team Drives
improves this for us.

00:24:49.470 --> 00:24:51.920
And it allows the team
to own the documents

00:24:51.920 --> 00:24:54.250
versus an individual.

00:24:54.250 --> 00:24:58.950
And so Team Drives helps us have
a better offboarding experience

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:04.950
and allows us to improve that
process in a more effective way

00:25:04.950 --> 00:25:08.311
so that the documents stay
with the organization.

00:25:11.020 --> 00:25:13.360
So in conclusion,
you can see how

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:16.150
we've done at the University--

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:20.200
focused on security,
performance, availability,

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:23.030
real-time collaboration,
and with team environments.

00:25:23.030 --> 00:25:25.450
The savings we gained
from moving to Google

00:25:25.450 --> 00:25:27.430
allowed us to
reinvest those funds

00:25:27.430 --> 00:25:29.110
into areas where
the student learning

00:25:29.110 --> 00:25:31.870
experience has made
the University one

00:25:31.870 --> 00:25:34.430
of the top places to attend.

00:25:34.430 --> 00:25:37.340
And going forward, we
want to be able to look

00:25:37.340 --> 00:25:40.130
at moving to an all-cloud
file storage solution

00:25:40.130 --> 00:25:42.920
where applicable and continue
the momentum of moving

00:25:42.920 --> 00:25:45.800
these files we
have to the cloud.

00:25:45.800 --> 00:25:48.950
We want to continue to develop
Team Drives in an environment

00:25:48.950 --> 00:25:54.170
for colleges and business units
to put their files in and have

00:25:54.170 --> 00:25:56.600
a better offboarding experience.

00:25:56.600 --> 00:25:59.330
As you can see, we
have 200 million files,

00:25:59.330 --> 00:26:01.250
and we're just getting started.

00:26:01.250 --> 00:26:04.040
Thank you, and I'll
hand it over to Josh.

00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:06.540
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:13.644 --> 00:26:15.310
JOSHUA HUMPHREY: Good
morning, everyone.

00:26:15.310 --> 00:26:17.880
My name is Josh Humphrey
from Georgia State University

00:26:17.880 --> 00:26:19.500
in Atlanta, Georgia.

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:21.360
And this morning I'm
going to share with you

00:26:21.360 --> 00:26:25.620
how Georgia State has
moved into the cloud

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:29.430
and how that move has greatly
improved our overall disaster

00:26:29.430 --> 00:26:30.840
recovery planning.

00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:33.300
Our main priority was, how
could we better protect

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:35.280
the University's
data without breaking

00:26:35.280 --> 00:26:37.540
the bank in the process?

00:26:37.540 --> 00:26:40.260
So many of you may
remember a small storm

00:26:40.260 --> 00:26:42.600
that happened last
year, Hurricane

00:26:42.600 --> 00:26:45.600
Irma, and the destruction
that it caused.

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:49.560
Atlanta, which normally does not
receive storms of this caliber,

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:52.570
was in the projected path
as it was entering the US.

00:26:56.310 --> 00:26:58.710
So since this was
our first major storm

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:01.100
that we were going
to face, luckily we

00:27:01.100 --> 00:27:02.850
had already started
moving our backup data

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:06.150
into Google Nearline storage.

00:27:06.150 --> 00:27:09.750
We knew this storm was going
to put GCP to the test,

00:27:09.750 --> 00:27:11.865
and we felt very
confident that we

00:27:11.865 --> 00:27:14.850
were going to be able
access our data in case

00:27:14.850 --> 00:27:16.440
the worst were to happen.

00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:20.670
So knowing this information,
we were able to not worry about

00:27:20.670 --> 00:27:23.730
how secure and how
safe our files were.

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:26.460
The whole team could then
focus on more pressing issues

00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:29.880
as we prepared for
the storm to hit.

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:32.550
Luckily, at the last
moment, the storm

00:27:32.550 --> 00:27:34.680
weakened and actually
changed course.

00:27:34.680 --> 00:27:37.830
So we did not have
any major outages.

00:27:37.830 --> 00:27:42.420
But it did help us prepare
for any future ones that

00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:43.420
may come down the road.

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:49.780
So our initial move to
the cloud was very small.

00:27:49.780 --> 00:27:53.020
We took very small baby steps.

00:27:53.020 --> 00:27:55.600
We had an initiative
several years back

00:27:55.600 --> 00:27:57.850
that was wanting us to
move some of our workloads

00:27:57.850 --> 00:27:59.170
into the cloud.

00:27:59.170 --> 00:28:04.570
So we decided to not focus on
major applications and systems.

00:28:04.570 --> 00:28:06.790
Instead, we decided
to keep it simple,

00:28:06.790 --> 00:28:11.920
and we targeted our 100-plus
terabytes of our backup data.

00:28:11.920 --> 00:28:13.960
The reason we
targeted this was we

00:28:13.960 --> 00:28:16.180
were the ownership
of the backup data.

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:20.770
So we didn't have to deal
with other application teams,

00:28:20.770 --> 00:28:24.190
and we didn't have to open
up a lot of extra security

00:28:24.190 --> 00:28:26.530
rules and stuff, working
with those teams.

00:28:26.530 --> 00:28:29.860
We could do it all in-house.

00:28:29.860 --> 00:28:32.890
So knowing that our backup
application actually

00:28:32.890 --> 00:28:36.220
supported multiple
connections to clouds,

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:40.600
we chose Google Nearline, and
we decided to start a pilot.

00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:43.780
So within a couple
of days of deciding

00:28:43.780 --> 00:28:47.980
we were going to do this pilot,
we created our GCP accounts.

00:28:47.980 --> 00:28:51.550
We then created our storage
buckets within Nearline.

00:28:51.550 --> 00:28:53.800
And we connected our
backup application

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:56.260
into the storage buckets.

00:28:56.260 --> 00:28:59.230
The next step was we created
a new storage policy,

00:28:59.230 --> 00:29:01.180
and we dumped a couple
of our dev systems

00:29:01.180 --> 00:29:03.250
into that storage
policy to have that data

00:29:03.250 --> 00:29:06.490
start shipping off to Nearline.

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:08.350
So for about two months,
we let that system

00:29:08.350 --> 00:29:13.150
run while we learned how
to manage this data--

00:29:15.710 --> 00:29:18.070
what was the process of
adding new systems, what

00:29:18.070 --> 00:29:21.740
was the process of restoring,
so on and so forth.

00:29:21.740 --> 00:29:26.290
So two of the main questions
that we were asking ourselves

00:29:26.290 --> 00:29:28.830
while we were doing this
two-month pilot was,

00:29:28.830 --> 00:29:32.620
was cloud going to work for
us for our disaster recovery

00:29:32.620 --> 00:29:34.300
planning for our backup data?

00:29:34.300 --> 00:29:39.340
And was Google going to be the
best option for hosting this?

00:29:39.340 --> 00:29:41.440
So after the two
months' timeframe,

00:29:41.440 --> 00:29:45.666
we found out how easy it was to
add more systems into Nearline.

00:29:45.666 --> 00:29:47.290
We found out how easy
it was to restore

00:29:47.290 --> 00:29:53.440
these systems, both at a file
level and a full server level.

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:56.350
And so at about eight
months, we were good.

00:29:56.350 --> 00:29:59.770
We decided to go all in and
pushed all of our backup data

00:29:59.770 --> 00:30:00.810
into Google Nearline.

00:30:04.660 --> 00:30:06.670
So now that we
were all in, it was

00:30:06.670 --> 00:30:10.000
time to toss our
security blanket aside.

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:14.080
And doing this saved us
a lot of time and money.

00:30:14.080 --> 00:30:16.130
And let me explain.

00:30:16.130 --> 00:30:19.780
So prior to getting rid
of our security blanket,

00:30:19.780 --> 00:30:23.510
we were using tapes
as our DR plan.

00:30:23.510 --> 00:30:26.890
So every day, our backup data
would be copied to tapes.

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:28.780
We had a dedicated
team in our data center

00:30:28.780 --> 00:30:31.930
who would go in,
rotate the tapes.

00:30:31.930 --> 00:30:34.540
We had a third party company
come in, pick up the tapes,

00:30:34.540 --> 00:30:36.970
send them off to a
secure remote site.

00:30:36.970 --> 00:30:39.640
If we ever needed
to restore anything,

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:41.800
we would contact that
company, hoping that we

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:43.386
requested the correct tapes.

00:30:43.386 --> 00:30:44.635
They would bring them back in.

00:30:44.635 --> 00:30:46.900
We would load them
into our tape library.

00:30:46.900 --> 00:30:51.970
And all that to just
start doing a restore.

00:30:51.970 --> 00:30:54.730
So we decided it was now time.

00:30:54.730 --> 00:30:57.010
We felt confident in
Google and the ability

00:30:57.010 --> 00:30:59.530
to restore this data
in case we needed it.

00:30:59.530 --> 00:31:01.990
So we decommissioned
that tape library

00:31:01.990 --> 00:31:06.850
and became 100% dependent
on GCP in case of disaster

00:31:06.850 --> 00:31:09.640
or any other type of emergency.

00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:12.570
So now that we no longer
had the tape library,

00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:15.320
we no longer had the other
hardware costs associated,

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:17.900
and the other third
party support,

00:31:17.900 --> 00:31:24.400
we saw a yearly
savings of around 44%.

00:31:24.400 --> 00:31:29.630
And we've been using GCP
for almost three years now,

00:31:29.630 --> 00:31:33.860
and we've seen this about
the same amount every year.

00:31:33.860 --> 00:31:36.910
So as Steve said,
in education, that

00:31:36.910 --> 00:31:41.170
is a huge amount when you're
dealing with budgets that

00:31:41.170 --> 00:31:44.890
aren't constant year to year.

00:31:44.890 --> 00:31:47.050
And because this is such
a significant number,

00:31:47.050 --> 00:31:49.480
we often use this
whenever people ask us,

00:31:49.480 --> 00:31:51.380
why did you move to the cloud?

00:31:51.380 --> 00:31:54.250
And we just bring this up
and say, this is our savings.

00:31:54.250 --> 00:31:57.350
So this is one of the
big reasons we moved--

00:31:57.350 --> 00:31:58.510
usability and the savings.

00:32:01.055 --> 00:32:03.500
Aside from the cost
savings, we also

00:32:03.500 --> 00:32:07.610
were able to reallocate about
10 of our data center workers

00:32:07.610 --> 00:32:10.697
into other areas of
the IT department

00:32:10.697 --> 00:32:12.530
where their skill sets
could be better used.

00:32:12.530 --> 00:32:15.710
So instead of working
on swapping out

00:32:15.710 --> 00:32:17.540
the tapes and everything,
those individuals

00:32:17.540 --> 00:32:20.640
could then go work
either at our help desk.

00:32:20.640 --> 00:32:22.760
Some of them went into
our server admin team,

00:32:22.760 --> 00:32:25.770
and some of them went into
our networking admin team.

00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:27.360
So it was a better
use of their time,

00:32:27.360 --> 00:32:29.590
better use of the
University's time and money

00:32:29.590 --> 00:32:33.170
having them moved out
and into the other areas

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:35.720
of the University.

00:32:35.720 --> 00:32:41.210
So now that we were all in
with GCP, with using Nearline,

00:32:41.210 --> 00:32:43.730
we were now able to reap
some of the benefits of using

00:32:43.730 --> 00:32:47.360
cloud services, three of
those being location, access,

00:32:47.360 --> 00:32:49.280
and growth.

00:32:49.280 --> 00:32:55.280
So being in Georgia, we
have a 30-mile requirement

00:32:55.280 --> 00:32:56.720
for our DR site.

00:32:56.720 --> 00:33:01.140
Google's data centers are far
beyond Georgia's boundaries,

00:33:01.140 --> 00:33:09.600
so we immediately gain
that check mark of,

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:13.820
is your data outside of a
certain mileage radius in case

00:33:13.820 --> 00:33:15.870
there was a disaster recovery?

00:33:15.870 --> 00:33:19.310
We also have the ability to a
simple configuration change,

00:33:19.310 --> 00:33:21.170
and we can then have
that data replicated

00:33:21.170 --> 00:33:25.650
to one of many other data
centers within the country.

00:33:25.650 --> 00:33:28.880
We also have near instant
access to our data.

00:33:28.880 --> 00:33:30.410
Like I mentioned
before, with tapes,

00:33:30.410 --> 00:33:35.150
we had to contact the
tape library company,

00:33:35.150 --> 00:33:36.680
have them ship the
tapes back to us

00:33:36.680 --> 00:33:39.410
before we could even
start that restore.

00:33:39.410 --> 00:33:41.690
Now, through our
backup application,

00:33:41.690 --> 00:33:43.580
a couple of clicks
and we started

00:33:43.580 --> 00:33:47.030
streaming our backups
back to our data center

00:33:47.030 --> 00:33:48.740
almost near instantly.

00:33:48.740 --> 00:33:52.220
And we also have the
ability to grow and expand

00:33:52.220 --> 00:33:54.020
our retention rates
for our backups

00:33:54.020 --> 00:33:56.360
without the cost
of extra hardware,

00:33:56.360 --> 00:33:59.720
without the cost of the extra
cooling in our data centers,

00:33:59.720 --> 00:34:02.040
and the electricity
that comes with that.

00:34:02.040 --> 00:34:04.070
So now within our
backup application,

00:34:04.070 --> 00:34:06.410
one configuration change
and we've expanded

00:34:06.410 --> 00:34:09.139
our overall retention rate.

00:34:09.139 --> 00:34:13.370
We can add days, weeks, months,
even make it indefinite,

00:34:13.370 --> 00:34:17.080
and it only costs us the monthly
storage bill from Google.

00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:23.290
So since we are
utilizing Nearline,

00:34:23.290 --> 00:34:26.590
we recognize that we're
still only scratching

00:34:26.590 --> 00:34:30.610
the surface of the services
that Google and using

00:34:30.610 --> 00:34:33.187
cloud technologies provides us.

00:34:33.187 --> 00:34:35.770
So one of the main things that
we're looking for in the future

00:34:35.770 --> 00:34:38.290
is, how can we get
to the point where

00:34:38.290 --> 00:34:40.360
we can utilize a lot
more of the services

00:34:40.360 --> 00:34:44.050
that Google provides, and
hopefully with the ability

00:34:44.050 --> 00:34:46.719
to one day just flip a switch
and have all of our mission

00:34:46.719 --> 00:34:50.920
critical systems come
online near instantly

00:34:50.920 --> 00:34:55.120
within GCP so we can continue
running our data center

00:34:55.120 --> 00:34:58.210
as seamlessly as
possible, and then

00:34:58.210 --> 00:35:01.174
see what other integrations
we might be able to utilize

00:35:01.174 --> 00:35:01.965
that Google offers.

00:35:04.970 --> 00:35:09.560
So in conclusion, my advice to
any of y'all in the audience

00:35:09.560 --> 00:35:13.910
who is looking to either start
your journey into using cloud

00:35:13.910 --> 00:35:16.670
technologies, or if you
want expand on your current

00:35:16.670 --> 00:35:20.540
environment, pretty much is to
just keep it simple and start

00:35:20.540 --> 00:35:23.070
with the small things.

00:35:23.070 --> 00:35:27.860
Since we had an initiative
and we decided to not focus

00:35:27.860 --> 00:35:30.680
on large applications-- we just
took small chunks of things

00:35:30.680 --> 00:35:34.070
that we knew we could get
those easy wins with--

00:35:34.070 --> 00:35:37.100
that's kind of the biggest
advice I can give to anybody,

00:35:37.100 --> 00:35:39.170
is we all probably
have a big picture

00:35:39.170 --> 00:35:41.270
of what we would like to
get to in the long run.

00:35:41.270 --> 00:35:44.239
But find those little
pieces of that picture,

00:35:44.239 --> 00:35:45.530
try to get successful in those.

00:35:45.530 --> 00:35:48.940
And then as you work
on those little pieces,

00:35:48.940 --> 00:35:50.960
the success rate
keeps going up and up,

00:35:50.960 --> 00:35:54.710
and then eventually you've
covered the whole picture

00:35:54.710 --> 00:35:58.320
that you were looking to do.

00:35:58.320 --> 00:35:59.690
And thank you.

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:01.790
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:01.790 --> 00:36:05.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

